INT. CORSET MAKER'S SHOP - BACK ROOM - NIGHT

1893. A single gas lamp. Bolts of silk. Whalebone stays arranged by length. A mannequin in half-finished brocade.

MADAME CLEMENTINE ROUSSEAU-ADEYEMI, 50s, pins fabric to the mannequin by lamplight. The shop bell rings in the front. She sighs, sets down her pins, and goes through the curtain.

INT. CORSET MAKER'S SHOP - FRONT ROOM - CONTINUOUS

A FIGURE stands inside the door, hat low, collar up, rain pooling on the boards. They hold a parcel wrapped in oilcloth.

FIGURE
Madame Rousseau?

CLEMENTINE
We are closed.

FIGURE
I know. I knocked at the back.

CLEMENTINE
Nobody knocks at the back.

FIGURE
I did.

She studies the Figure. The stance is wary. The voice is low, uncertain of its register.

CLEMENTINE
What do you want?

FIGURE
A fitting.

CLEMENTINE
At this hour?

FIGURE
I could not come in daylight.

Clementine's brow furrows. She looks at the parcel. The Figure steps forward, unwraps it, lifts out a gentleman's frock coat, torn at the seam.

FIGURE (CONT'D)
My brother's. He died on the fourteenth. I'd like it altered.

CLEMENTINE
For whom?

FIGURE
For me.

A long silence. The gas lamp hisses. Clementine takes a step closer. Her expression softens.

CLEMENTINE
You will not need a corset, then.

FIGURE
(a small, relieved breath)
No. Only a good tailor.

CLEMENTINE
Then you came to the wrong shop.

FIGURE
Forgive me.

CLEMENTINE
No, no. You came to the right one. I am not a tailor. But I know three.

She takes the coat gently. Runs her thumb over the torn seam.

CLEMENTINE (CONT'D)
What is your name?

FIGURE
(after a beat)
Ambrose.

CLEMENTINE
Ambrose what?

AMBROSE
Ambrose Whitlock. For now.

CLEMENTINE
For now is enough. Sit, Monsieur Whitlock. I will make some tea.
